[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Management for Aggregations (Fielddata and Doc Values)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Elasticsearch, aggregations, sorting, and parent-child relationships require access to raw field values. To perform these operations efficiently, Elasticsearch uses two primary data structures: Fielddata and Doc Values. Understanding how they manage memory is crucial for cluster stability and performance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Doc Values"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Doc Values are the default on-disk data structure for all fields except analyzed text fields. Because they reside on disk, they do not consume JVM heap memory. Operating system file system caches handle caching doc values for high performance."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Fielddata"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Fielddata loads all field values into the JVM heap memory to enable fast in-memory aggregations and sorting. This is required primarily for text fields that have been analyzed (tokenized). However, because fielddata is unmanaged by default, excessive usage can lead to OutOfMemory (OOM) errors."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Monitoring Heap Usage"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can monitor fielddata memory consumption using the node stats API. Look specifically at the indices.fielddata.memory_size metric."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"GET /_nodes/stats/indices/fielddata"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Fielddata Circuit Breaker"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent fielddata from crashing the JVM, Elasticsearch utilizes a circuit breaker. If an aggregation attempts to load more memory than allowed, the query is aborted and an exception is thrown."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"PUT /_cluster/settings
{
  "persistent": {
    "indices.breaker.fielddata.limit": "40%"
  }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Memory Management"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid running aggregations or sorting on analyzed text fields. Use keyword fields instead, which leverage Doc Values."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set appropriate JVM heap sizes, typically not exceeding 50% of total system RAM, leaving the remaining 50% for the OS file system cache."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enable doc values wherever possible and disable fielddata entirely on fields that do not require it."}]
